<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Viti Levu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Viti Levu is the largest island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, covering about 10,400 square kilometres and holding most of the country's population. The capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suva">Suva</a>, lies on its south-east coast and the main international airport at Nadi in the west. Off its eastern shore lies the islet of Bau, from which the chiefs of Bau dominated much of Fiji in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/viti-levu.jpg" alt="Viti Levu" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vitilevu_topo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name Viti Levu means Great Fiji. The island is 146 kilometres long and 106 kilometres wide. Fiji lies in a tectonically complex area between the Australian and Pacific plates, and earthquakes and volcanic eruptions account for the rugged terrain. A mountain range running north to south divides the island into roughly equal halves, and the forested centre includes the nation's highest peak, Mount Tomanivi, also called Mount Victoria, at 1,324 metres. Heavy rain falls on the eastern side, which has Fiji's tropical moist forests. The western side, colloquially called the burning west, lies in the range's rain shadow and is drier, with tropical dry forests and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Viti Levu is believed to have been inhabited longer than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanua-levu">Vanua Levu</a>, the island to its north. According to oral tradition, the first Melanesian settlers landed at Vuda Point and founded Viseisei as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>'s oldest settlement, though archaeological evidence contradicts this account. Many of the ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-fijians">Indo-Fijians</a>, who are concentrated in the west, came to the island from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> as indentured workers between 1879 and 1916. Indigenous Fijians predominate in the east, except in the cities, which are more mixed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Viti Levu holds the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suva">Suva</a>, and a large majority of the country's people. Other important towns spread along the coast include Ba, Lautoka, Nadi, Nausori, Rakiraki and Sigatoka. Eight of Fiji's fourteen provinces lie on the island. A single major road runs around its perimeter, and Nadi International Airport on the north-west side is the principal airport of Fiji. The island is the only known home of the Giant Fijian long-horned beetle, one of the world's largest insects.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Viti Levu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bau is an islet of a few hectares off the east coast of <b>Viti Levu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bau">Kingdom of Bau</a></li></ul></section>
